# Sprint 28a (1b, M13) — the aborts Windows is ALLOWED to have, by REASON.
#
# Each line is a substring; every abort the suite reports must match one.
# An abort matching nothing FAILS the job.
#
# PROVENANCE: MEASURED on CI run 31852349674, the first Windows run of this
# branch. The list started as patterns derived from the source's assumption
# messages; three more were added from what Windows actually reported, which is
# the gate working as intended on its first outing.

# The embedding corpus is a large artifact not carried in the repository.
no corpus at -Djawata.embed.corpus

# The profiler ranks SAMPLED measurements; under contention it can return too
# few samples to rank anything, and refuses to assert on a coin toss.
CPU profile too thin

# The RCP fixture stands in for a NATIVE eclipse launcher. A .cmd cannot exec
# the way the POSIX fake does, so the held JVM sits behind a cmd.exe the attach
# wait never times out on — the first attempt burned 75 runner-minutes before
# CI killed it (run 31789882384). An honest skip beats that.
the RCP launcher fake needs exec semantics a Windows .cmd cannot express

# A .cmd stand-in does not receive argv intact under ProcessBuilder's cmd.exe
# wrapping: the batch twin scans its whole argv for the -Dmdep.outputFile
# prefix and exits 3 on a miss, which run 31804158929 shows exactly. The
# sibling tests needing NO argv pass on Windows, so the invocation path itself
# is proven there. The java-launcher fake that removes this skip is M11.
the .cmd fake does not receive argv under cmd.exe wrapping

# --- MEASURED on the first Windows run (CI 31852349674) ---

# The matrix jobs deliberately set -Djawata.skip.gradle=true; the separate
# gradle-cell job is where these must NOT skip. See expected-aborts.linux.
Gradle Tooling API tests skipped via jawata.skip.gradle=true

# Native crash triage parses hs_err in the POSIX dialect. Windows writes a
# different one (EXCEPTION_ACCESS_VIOLATION, a .mdmp beside the log), and the
# tests say so rather than asserting against text they cannot read. The
# dialect itself is DEFERRED to the Sprint-28-family native-triage batch; the
# boundary already places its seams (HostFs.crashLogs keeps the minidump out
# of the hs_err set), so the feature slots in without moving them.
hs_err parsing speaks the POSIX dialect

# A guard test needs a directory the process cannot read. The Windows runner's
# user ignores the read bit, so the precondition cannot be established — and
# the test refuses to pass a guard it never actually exercised.
cannot make a directory unreadable
